 ### [Regular Grid Traversal](5939-regular-grid-traversal.md)
-Regular Grid Traversal is a ray-scene intersection acceleration technique that partitions a scene bounding box into a fixed-size 3D grid of cells, returning the sequence of cells intersected by a ray to minimize primitive intersection tests. It solves the problem of testing every object for every ray by only testing primitives stored in intersected cells. Unlike hierarchical structures (e.g., BVH, Octree) that partition primitives into disjoint sets, the Regular Grid partitions space into disjoint sub-blocks, allowing primitives to belong to multiple sub-blocks. It is distinguished by its constant-time access to cells and simple traversal logic, making it optimal for scenes with uniform data distribution or dynamic scenes requiring frequent rebuilds.
